Fungal skin infections (e.g.Intertrigo/Candidiasis)
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Beefy-red intertriginous erythema, Satellite papules/pustules, Maceration/fissuring, Intense localized burning

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-022
Reason for Visit: Acute severe skin pain, fissuring, and suspected secondary bacterial infection.
Location: Nursing home
Present at Visit: Patient and nursing staff
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an immobile, bedbound patient who suddenly developed severe, agonizing pain and bleeding from the groin and abdominal skin folds over the last 12 hours, refusing all personal care. Assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the high risk of deep tissue cellulitis, the severe distress inhibiting essential hygiene, and the need for immediate prescription of combined topical and systemic therapy.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My skin is on fire down there, it feels like it is tearing open every time I move.
History of Present Illness: The nursing staff report a 3-day history of increasing redness in the deep skin folds under the abdomen and in the bilateral groins, exacerbated by urinary incontinence and prolonged moisture. Over the past 12 hours, the patient reports intense localized pruritus progressing to a severe burning sensation. Staff noted maceration and painful fissuring in the depths of the intertriginous areas earlier today, which are now bleeding upon movement. The patient has significant mobility limitations and is entirely bedbound, contributing to excessive sweating, friction, and poor wound healing in the occluded areas.
Functional Status: Bedbound; requires maximal assistance for all turning and hygiene.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe pain limiting compliance with turning schedules, increasing the risk of decubitus ulcers.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 37.4 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 110.0 Kg
General: Patient is visibly distressed, grimacing during any movement, and fiercely guarding the pelvic region.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam reveals bright beefy-red erythematous patches in the bilateral inguinal folds and the infraumbilical pannus fold. There is a classic presence of characteristic satellite papules and pustules extending beyond the main advancing border of the erythema. Deep, linear, bleeding fissures are present at the base of the folds. A white, curd-like exudate is visible in the deepest crevices, accompanied by a distinct malodour. Surrounding skin is warm but lacks the deep, indurated swelling of frank cellulitis.
Environmental Observations: Incontinence pad is heavily saturated; room temperature is excessively warm, promoting diaphoresis.
Medication Reconciliation: No current topical antifungal use; patient is on broad-spectrum antibiotics for a recent UTI, predisposing to candidal overgrowth.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Fungal skin infections (e.g.Intertrigo/Candidiasis) (with severe maceration and painful fissuring) - Status: Acute complication
Differentials:

Bacterial Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the strictly superficial nature of the erythema, lack of deep tissue induration, absence of high fever, and the pathognomonic presence of satellite pustules outside the main border.

Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of the white curd-like exudate and satellite lesions, which specifically point to an infectious yeast process rather than a pure chemical irritation from urine.

Inverse Psoriasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the acute onset, severe maceration, and lack of typical psoriatic plaques on extensor surfaces or nail pitting.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of severe, burning skin pain and bleeding fissures in occluded skin folds constitutes a severe candidal intertrigo exacerbated by immobility and recent antibiotic use. The objective findings of beefy-red erythema, satellite pustules, and deep maceration confirmed the diagnosis. This assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period because the intense pain was actively preventing essential nursing care and repositioning, creating an immediate risk for rapid development of deep pressure injuries and secondary bacterial sepsis. Immediate medical intervention was required to initiate definitive antifungal therapy and implement strict moisture-control protocols.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Cleaned the fissures gently with normal saline. Prescribed Clotrimazole 1% / Hydrocortisone 1% compounded cream to treat the fungal load and rapidly reduce the intense inflammatory burning. Prescribed a 7-day course of oral Fluconazole due to the extensive surface area and depth of the fissures.
Positive Behavioral Change: When staff apply a thick zinc-based barrier cream immediately after the antifungal absorbs, it blocks urine from touching the raw skin; therefore, making this a strict two-step protocol during every pad change ensures the fissures can heal. When you ask the nurses to place rolled dry towels under your skin folds for 30 minutes twice a day, you allow the skin to breathe; cooperating with this air-drying routine directly stops the yeast from growing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a wound care nurse specialist to monitor the deep fissures.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommended a high-absorbency, breathable incontinence pad system.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to ensure the fissures are granulating and erythema is receding.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops a high fever, if the redness rapidly spreads down the thighs, or if the skin develops black, necrotic patches.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Fungal infections in deep folds are highly prone to recurrence if moisture is not continuously controlled.
Clinical Course: The hydrocortisone will relieve the burning within 24 hours. The fissures will take 7-10 days to completely close with strict dryness and oral antifungals.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Nursing staff must monitor for increasing warmth or hard swelling under the skin. Seek urgent medical review if the pain worsens despite the new creams.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The recent antibiotics killed your good bacteria, allowing a yeast naturally on your skin to overgrow in the warm, moist folds. The oral pill works from the inside out to kill the yeast, while the cream reduces the severe burning and targets the local area.
Actionable Steps: Keep the skin folds meticulously dry. Allow air-drying twice a day. Apply the prescribed creams exactly as directed before the barrier cream.

